WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.239
Welcome to week four.

2
00:00:01.289 --> 00:00:04.786
The theme: if you can't measure it, you haven't built it.

3
00:00:04.836 --> 00:00:16.392
By the end of this week your team will have an evaluation report, and this video gives you its two foundations: a golden set, and a metric called pass^k, written pass, caret, k.

4
00:00:17.142 --> 00:00:21.748
A golden set is a fixed list of tasks with known correct outcomes.

5
00:00:21.798 --> 00:00:32.491
Each case has three parts: the input, a description of what done looks like, and a way to check it, by exact match, by a rule, or by a person.

6
00:00:32.541 --> 00:00:33.923
Your project needs twenty.

7
00:00:33.973 --> 00:00:35.714
Start with ten this week.

8
00:00:35.764 --> 00:00:37.504
Where do cases come from?

9
00:00:37.554 --> 00:00:38.578
Real requests, anonymised.

10
00:00:38.628 --> 00:00:41.443
The failures you have already seen in traces.

11
00:00:41.493 --> 00:00:47.889
And edge cases: an empty input, a question with two valid answers, a request the agent should refuse.

12
00:00:47.939 --> 00:00:51.470
A golden set made only of easy cases measures nothing.

13
00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:54.711
Why not just run each case once?

14
00:00:54.761 --> 00:00:56.526
Because agents are not deterministic.

15
00:00:56.576 --> 00:01:00.157
The same input can take a different path next time.

16
00:01:00.207 --> 00:01:07.417
The clearest demonstration is τ-bench, from June 2024, a benchmark of customer-service tasks with a simulated user and real tools.

17
00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:13.225
The best agents of the time passed fewer than half the tasks on a single run.

18
00:01:13.275 --> 00:01:20.123
Asked to pass the same task eight times in a row, they managed it on fewer than a quarter.

19
00:01:20.173 --> 00:01:24.479
The paper introduced pass^k to make that gap visible.

20
00:01:25.229 --> 00:01:26.445
Here is the arithmetic.

21
00:01:26.495 --> 00:01:29.926
Suppose an agent passes a task 90 percent of the time.

22
00:01:29.975 --> 00:01:34.356
Pass to the k is the chance it passes k runs in a row.

23
00:01:34.406 --> 00:01:35.938
For two runs, 81 percent.

24
00:01:35.988 --> 00:01:37.204
For five, 59 percent.

25
00:01:37.254 --> 00:01:38.469
For eight, 43 percent.

26
00:01:38.519 --> 00:01:45.748
A customer who uses the agent once a day for a working week sees a clean week less than six times in ten.

27
00:01:45.798 --> 00:01:50.494
That is the number a manager needs, and it is the number a demo hides.

28
00:01:50.544 --> 00:02:02.203
So for every case in your golden set you run the agent k times, at least three, and you report two numbers: the share of runs that passed, and the share of cases that passed every time.

29
00:02:02.953 --> 00:02:04.581
What counts as a pass?

30
00:02:04.631 --> 00:02:08.944
Anthropic's January 2026 guide to agent evals makes a distinction you should copy.

31
00:02:08.994 --> 00:02:12.972
Grade the outcome: did the state of the world end up right?

32
00:02:13.022 --> 00:02:18.342
The refund was issued for the right amount; the file exists and contains the right table.

33
00:02:18.392 --> 00:02:24.048
And grade the path when it matters: which tools were called, in what order, with what arguments.

34
00:02:24.098 --> 00:02:30.760
An agent that reached the right answer by calling a tool it was not supposed to call did not pass.

35
00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:37.473
The trace is where you check the path, which is why every case in your harness should save its trace.

36
00:02:38.223 --> 00:02:40.934
So here is the skeleton of your evaluation report.

37
00:02:40.984 --> 00:02:44.309
The golden set itself, twenty cases with the check for each.

38
00:02:44.359 --> 00:02:47.070
The number of runs per case, at least three.

39
00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.672
The pass rate per run and pass^k per case, side by side.

40
00:02:51.722 --> 00:02:55.660
The gap between the two numbers tells you how consistent the agent is.

41
00:02:55.710 --> 00:03:00.262
And the list of failed cases with a link to the trace of each failure.

42
00:03:00.312 --> 00:03:03.637
The next video is about what to do with those failures.

43
00:03:03.687 --> 00:03:09.160
It is called error analysis, and it is the most useful hour your team will spend this term.
